# net: the network for programs, served by netstack (user/services/netstack)
# on /svc/net. A program whose list asks for it (`svc net`) finds it there;
# init publishes it and keeps the shared channel's server end across
# netstack's restarts. Each opener (libos's svc_open, svc_get) gets a
# channel of its own with the svc protocol's connect (abi/idl/svc.idl): a
# reply that comes later (an echo, a wait) goes to that caller's channel
# only. The shared channel itself answers connect, `iface` and `counts`.
# The same protocol is served on a second shared channel, /svc/net-sys,
# which init gives only the network's own services (dns, netlog, update;
# never a program from /data): its openers are SYSTEM openers, which may
# use a reserve of netstack's openers, sockets, waits and ring bytes that
# ordinary programs' openers can't (<net.h> NET_PROG_*).
# <net.h> has the client side (blocking helpers for programs, forms that
# don't wait for services' loops) and the limits; docs/M9-PLAN.md
# "Programs and sockets" and docs/M9.5-PLAN.md the design.
#
# Addresses are IPv4 addresses as numbers, the first byte highest
# (10.2.21.5 is 0x0a021505); ports are numbers. A datagram is at most
# NET_DGRAM_MAX (1472) bytes: one Ethernet frame, never fragmented.
#
# Two kinds of channel speak this protocol:
# - an opener's channel: iface, wait_change, counts, chip_counts, udp,
#   udp_rings, echo. At most NET_OPENERS openers (connect: ERR_NO_RESOURCES;
#   NET_PROG_OPENERS of them ordinary ones). Closing it closes its sockets
#   too.
# - a socket's channel (from `udp` or `udp_rings`, or netctl's dhcp_open):
#   the sock_* methods. Closing it closes the socket.
# A method sent to the other kind of channel is ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED.
#
# A socket's datagrams travel through its rings (<sockring.h>: one VMO
# with a tx and an rx ring, and two events), never in a call: udp_rings
# makes a socket with its rings, sock_rings gives a socket its rings.
#
# Limits, so one program can't use netstack up (<net.h>):
# NET_SOCKETS_PER_OPENER (16) sockets an opener, NET_SOCKETS_MAX (48) in
# all, NET_PROG_SOCKETS (24) of them ordinary openers'; NET_LATER_PER_OPENER
# (8) waits, echoes and chip_counts in flight an opener; ring bytes
# (sockring_bytes) SOCKRING_OPENER_BYTES an opener, SOCKRING_TOTAL_BYTES in
# all, NET_PROG_RING_BYTES of them ordinary openers'. A datagram that
# doesn't fit a socket's rx ring is dropped and counted (netstack never
# waits for a reader).
#
# Reconnect: when netstack ends, every opener's and socket's channel sees
# ERR_PEER_CLOSED; open /svc/net again (svc_get does), whose connect waits
# (bounded by the caller's deadline) on the shared channel, which init
# keeps, for the restarted netstack; then open the sockets again. A new
# netstack starts with no address until init or the DHCP client sets it
# (wait_change).
# Format: see tools/genidl.py.

protocol net 29

# On an opener's channel (or the shared one): the interface. Its address,
# mask and gateway (0: none), the DNS servers (0: none), its MAC address;
# `device` 1 while a network card's driver has a session with netstack,
# `link` 1 while its link is up; the VLAN every frame is tagged with (0
# without a card), the link speed in Mb/s (0 while down); `version` counts
# the changes to the address, gateway and DNS servers since netstack
# started (1 at the start): pass it to wait_change.
1  iface  ()  -> (u32 address, u32 mask, u32 gateway, u32 dns1, u32 dns2, u8[6] mac, u8 device, u8 link, u16 vlan, u32 speed, u32 version)
# On an opener's channel: answered when iface's `version` is no longer
# `version` (at once if it isn't), with the new one; ERR_TIMED_OUT after
# timeout_ms (NET_WAIT_FOREVER: no timeout). How a program waits for an
# address (<net.h> net_wait_up) or for new DNS servers.
2  wait_change  (u32 version, u32 timeout_ms)  -> (u32 version) later
# On an opener's channel (or the shared one): netstack's counts, as
# <net.h>'s struct net_counters (little-endian, its reserved fields 0).
3  counts  ()  -> (u8[256] counts)
# On an opener's channel: the network card's own counts, as the driver
# answers netdev.stats (<jam/netdev.h> struct netdev_stats). ERR_NOT_FOUND:
# no session with a card's driver; ERR_PEER_CLOSED: the session ended
# before the driver answered.
4  chip_counts  ()  -> (u8[256] counts) later
# On an opener's channel: a UDP socket on local port `port`, any local
# address, without its rings yet (sock_rings gives them). port 0: netstack
# picks one (49152 and up). Results: `socket`, a channel of its own that
# speaks the sock_* methods (closing it closes the socket), and its port.
# ERR_ACCESS_DENIED: a port below 1024 (only netctl's DHCP socket has
# one), or one below 49152 on an opener that didn't come through
# /svc/net-listen (the listen permission, netstack's listen.h);
# ERR_ALREADY_BOUND: the port is taken; ERR_NO_RESOURCES: the opener has
# NET_SOCKETS_PER_OPENER sockets, NET_SOCKETS_MAX are open, or an
# ordinary opener's NET_PROG_SOCKETS are.
5  udp  (u16 port)  -> (handle socket, u16 port)
# On an opener's channel: an ICMP echo request (a ping) to `address` with
# `size` bytes of data (0..NET_DGRAM_MAX) and sequence number `seq`, from
# the opener's own echo id (netstack picks it: no opener sees another's
# replies). Answered when the reply comes: the round trip in microseconds,
# the reply's TTL and its data's size; ERR_TIMED_OUT after timeout_ms
# (1..60000); ERR_NOT_FOUND: a router or the host said it is unreachable
# (ICMP destination unreachable). At once: ERR_INVALID_ARGS (not a unicast
# address, a size or timeout out of range), ERR_BAD_STATE (no address, the
# link down, no route; or an echo with this seq to this address is in
# flight), ERR_NO_RESOURCES (NET_LATER_PER_OPENER in flight already).
6  echo  (u32 address, u16 seq, u16 size, u32 timeout_ms)  -> (u32 rtt_us, u8 ttl, u16 size) later
# On an opener's channel: udp(port) and the socket's sock_rings(tx_bytes,
# rx_bytes) in one call: the socket's channel, its rings' VMO and events
# (<sockring.h>: SOCKRING_VMO_RIGHTS, SOCKRING_TO_STACK_RIGHTS,
# SOCKRING_TO_PROG_RIGHTS; datagram framing), its port and the rings'
# sizes. The errors of both.
15  udp_rings  (u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)  -> (handle socket, handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)

# Ordinals 16 and 17 are not used again.

# On a socket's channel: from now on only datagrams from address:port go
# into its rx ring (others are dropped, not counted as dropped), and a tx
# record to 0:0 goes there. Address 0 and port 0: any sender again.
# Datagrams in the ring already stay. ERR_INVALID_ARGS: an address a
# program can't send to; ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED on the DHCP socket.
18  sock_connect  (u32 address, u16 port)  -> ()
# On a socket's channel: its local port, the peer sock_connect set (0: none),
# bytes waiting in its rx ring now, and datagrams dropped since it opened
# (its rx ring was full; also the status line's rx_dropped).
19  sock_state  ()  -> (u16 port, u32 peer, u16 peer_port, u32 queued, u32 dropped)
# On a socket's channel: the socket's rings (<sockring.h>, datagram
# framing): their VMO and events with the rights the header names, and
# the rings' sizes. tx_bytes and rx_bytes: 0 for SOCKRING_UDP_TX and
# SOCKRING_UDP_RX, else a ring size (sockring_size_ok). After it the
# socket's datagrams go only through the rings: a tx record netstack
# refuses (an address a program can't send to, port 0, no route) counts in
# the status line's tx_refused, with its reason in `error`, and a datagram
# that doesn't fit the rx ring in rx_dropped. Once a socket: ERR_BAD_STATE
# for a second. ERR_INVALID_ARGS: a size not allowed; ERR_NO_RESOURCES:
# over the opener's or all sockets' ring bytes; ERR_NO_MEMORY: no memory.
20  sock_rings  (u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)  -> (handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)
